#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "ae470aa4-327d-5b21-9e11-217710f1ef94" :question/math-academy-id "e-13787" :question/problem "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$, we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\div {\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "0b51ee70-1d44-5427-8b9f-3930dc3a1962" :question/math-academy-id "e-13790" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n![](images/e8/e8b1d1d44a8b8b08fa31061f8a87e632a8422f3494d77d9b6d8ba02a446c3e65.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:  \n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\div {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/583d7875ca78bb24742c402e222b4ba221388163d17f2056372e204d27373043.svg)\n\nHence, the missing number is $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/id #uuid "daa61f81-4882-5af9-b86a-8b03a586e93b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13792" :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $3.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 3 = 12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "066a9329-d63e-55d4-bc6b-bcdb5dd96cc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13793" :question/problem "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $7$ and $14.$ We can make a common denominator of $14.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\div {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d471d0f-464f-55dd-b8eb-64d49513596c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458645} {:answer/id #uuid "584df28f-bd81-5332-a82c-29f559cd0f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458778} {:answer/id #uuid "60b761ec-46fe-5441-82bb-092473870ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458791} {:answer/id #uuid "f2edc7ec-efd9-52e5-aa79-bba8a05c5928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459056} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f42d21-26eb-5350-a63f-81bfc9ebd0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584df28f-bd81-5332-a82c-29f559cd0f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458778} :answer-field/id #uuid "46797552-3a8c-5cdf-8ade-bfb959a128ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "759e1569-9d6b-53f0-909f-590a2bb56cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100624" :question/problem "![](images/38/38b8cedf7155418e898f4bbd1c5aefd4c9be7bc8fa93efbd6b15042bc2177cb3.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{9}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically: \n\n![](images/97/9792dd036c4de182d4cd491830bbca85d083b7b19e80c356e67615d8f00db230.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{10}÷\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n8÷9=\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,8\\,}}{9}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc8bca5-fccc-546d-b738-2d0c86b4b7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458682} {:answer/id #uuid "790f1fa8-47d3-5d58-b3c9-b37562f4c20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458843} {:answer/id #uuid "80e9f6f5-0c7a-56a2-80b1-a45bdaa63fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458855} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e90688-8a2b-58fc-ae28-584b2349b527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459020} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e5a7ab-e2fa-51e5-ab94-fb62ca901c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790f1fa8-47d3-5d58-b3c9-b37562f4c20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458843} :answer-field/id #uuid "2193b56d-ba94-5c35-b16b-52ed5a3cdd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8619bd8f-8cd6-5dfe-88b7-344fe25b3ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-100723" :question/problem "![](images/de/de7182658c41542b8e6ef212aa1896204c4c4fe07a79c8ef93f85012188684d6.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{2}{4}$ given the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $8$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $8.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/f9/f9bb3e4777085c1e02c4965c41972f5e95261b1228f84b567d049bee8bbe139e.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{4}{8}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n3÷4=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b011bf75-8904-5fbb-9e8a-16c5b9853b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b011bf75-8904-5fbb-9e8a-16c5b9853b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458943} :answer-field/id #uuid "77b31b38-bca3-5d91-a0e2-46f4afd4e423" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edc03b89-64bc-5e65-83ba-37357e64a9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-174092" :question/problem "![](images/72/72f5c8ef7e4ee3beb67bb8ebdbe573da7718161cd4378064d160bbfc10affbee.png)\n\nUse the model above to compute the following result. Express your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n$\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{5}{7}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f566a58700fa3c0671e4d1ebdd7609a7691ed7a269991be47344ce8afcebeac8.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{5}{7}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{5}{7}$ fits into $\\frac{3}{7}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $5$ fits into $3.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{5}{7}$, we can simply compute $3÷5.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n3÷5=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$"}] 